            <ArticleTitle>[Myocardial infarction-like electrocardiogram in acute Hantavirus infection. Suspected secondary peri-myocarditis].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1240-3</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1055/s-2008-1077246</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="HISTORY AND ADMISSION FINDINGS" NlmCategory="METHODS">A 19-year-old man was admitted to our hospital because of fever and exhaustion. During his hospital stay he had pain in the back and flanks.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="INVESTIGATIONS" NlmCategory="METHODS">The electrocardiogram (ECG) revealed ST-segment elevation in V1-V3, similar to that occurring in myocardial infarction. Renal function was decreased with proteinuria and the platelet count was decreased The symptoms and clinical findings aroused the suspicion of a Hantavirus infection, which was confirmed by antibody tests.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="TREATMENT AND COURSE" NlmCategory="METHODS">The patient was treated symptomatically with paracetamol. After 5 days the patient felt better and had a normal temperature. The thrombocyte count and renal function normalised after 10 days in hospital.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">In case of a suspected peril-/ myocarditis a Hantavirus infection should be considered as a possible cause, which should be clarified by antibody tests.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
